{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790538822,"nonce":"IVyRVLh5JSUhhwFCBOx0Ioiu+y4=","message":"Fixed in 3f59660a06816991a00482d615e148b68ffe3661 with f55cd25f522b07d152c3ee8b24b4ed1cd0437a8a and Ash a077d38. A Wren function crosses a linked call as a Haxe closure of the declared type (caribou_wren_to_haxe_function / from_haxe_function, cast for TypeRef::Function). On wasm Ash gives no record closure, so the closure is the var-args form and compiled Haxe calls it through its dynamic path; Ash's call sites send a closure whose type is not the site's through hlp_dyn_call, so no per-signature trampoline is needed for this. The linked program now enters its Wren VM at start, which the call into Wren needed. Separately, caribou_abi's vclosure had stackCount on 32-bit targets, which hl.h does not, so a Wren function returned from Haxe was not recognised as itself (f55cd25f522b07d152c3ee8b24b4ed1cd0437a8a). Checked on wasm32-wasip1: Haxe calls a Wren Fn (f(41) = 42), loops through it (Bench.wrenClosure), Wren calls a Haxe closure (apply(x -\u003e x * 2, 21) = 42), and a Wren Fn round-trips through Haxe as itself. The wasm bench's Haxe→Wren closure cell reads 478.9 ns; the boxed path's cost is 9234beb.","files":null}]}